### Abstract    
This week, my focus shifted to runtime analysis and algorithm optimization for all bivariate depth functions I have implemented so far. I began by building a standard framework to test how my algorithms scale as $n \to \infty$, but quickly encountered severe execution bottlenecks. To understand what was slowing my code down under the hood, I turned to The R Inferno by Patrick Burns [@burns2011r] and systematically improved the runtime by pre-allocating memory, eliminating matrix binding, and replacing 'for' loops with vectorized operations. By isolating CPU execution time and applying local polynomial regression (loess) to filter out hardware noise, I confirmed that my empirical curves match their theoretical runtime expectation. Ultimately, this refactoring dropped the processing time for a dataset of 1,000,000 points to under 0.4 seconds which was a massive win for me. 

